    {
      "dom_id": "question-60640",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Nicole bakes triangular cakes with a base of $5$ cm and a height of $3$ cm. What is the area of one cake?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch our cake based on the description.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14199152/assets/205df21c66b54b84f9b0608baebf49277720738e1fac14219a7631848ea5c19a.png)\n\nThe height and base of the triangle are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{base} & =5, \\\\ \\text{height} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle can be found by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}=\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\text{base}\\times \\text{height}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values of the base and height, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times 5\\times 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\times 15 \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the units. Since base and height are measured in centimeters, the area is measured in square centimeters. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}=7.5\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
